  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon_bifasciatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Yellow Tetra is a peaceful schooling characin from coastal rivers in southeast Brazil. It has low requirements for food and water chemistry and is recommended for beginners in a community aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon_bifasciatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Yellow Tetra originates, like its close relative the Von Rio Tetra, from coastal rivers in southeast Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon_bifasciatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Their colours shine particularly in aquariums that are not too brightly lit and have a dark substrate. They need a free swimming area, and the border regions of the aquarium should be densely planted.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon_bifasciatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>As a schooling fish the Yellow Tetra should be kept only in larger swarms.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon_bifasciatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>They are suitable for first steps in characin breeding, since they already spawn in water with a slightly acidic pH and moderate hardness, and raising the fry is not too difficult.</p>"
    }
  ],
